  'how.sourceTime': 'newest source count: {t}',
  'how.senate.1': 'We show the 35 contests of 2026: 33 from “Class II” (six-year terms ending January 2027) plus two special elections, in Florida and Ohio, finishing terms of senators who left office.',
  'how.senate.2': 'The other 15 states do not elect a senator in 2026: their seats belong to other classes. They show as “No race”.',
  'how.senate.3': 'The Senate has 100 seats. The 65 not on the ballot stay as they were (senate.gov) and show in the bar as “Not up”. A majority is 51; on a 50–50 tie the vice president breaks it.',
  'how.senate.4': 'Votes and percentages come from the source, per candidate; a candidate’s percentage is of the votes counted so far. “% reporting” is the share of units (precincts, counties or towns, depending on the state) that have reported: it is not the share of votes.',
  'how.senate.5': 'Leading only says who has the most votes right now. “Projected” is an outlet’s estimate, which we cite with a link. “Official” comes from state authorities and can still change. “Certified” requires the state’s document. A 100% count is none of the three.',
  'how.senate.6': 'Awaiting count = a source covers the state but no votes are published. Data unavailable = the contest exists but no integrated source covers it (we link the state’s official site). No race = there is no election. These are three different things.',
  'how.governor.1': 'We show the 36 governor elections of 2026. In some states the ballot has tickets (governor/lieutenant governor), shown as the state publishes them, for example “Donalds / Avila”.',
  'how.governor.2': 'The other 14 states elect governors in other years and show as “No race”.',
  'how.governor.3': 'Each contest fills a single office; there are no seats to add up. The summary counts declared wins and leads by party, without projecting who “will win”.',
  'how.governor.4': 'Votes and percentages come from the source, per candidate; a candidate’s percentage is of the votes counted so far. “% reporting” is the share of units (precincts, counties or towns, depending on the state) that have reported: it is not the share of votes.',
  'how.governor.5': 'Leading only says who has the most votes right now. “Projected” is an outlet’s estimate, which we cite with a link. “Official” comes from state authorities and can still change. “Certified” requires the state’s document. A 100% count is none of the three.',
  'how.governor.6': 'Awaiting count = a source covers the state but no votes are published. Data unavailable = the contest exists but no integrated source covers it (we link the state’s official site). No race = there is no election. These are three different things.',
  'how.house.1': 'We show all 435 seats, one per congressional district, all up every two years. Alaska, Delaware, North Dakota, South Dakota, Vermont and Wyoming have a single (“at-large”) district.',
  'how.house.2': 'Each district has its own result and color. On the state map, the strip shows the delegation’s makeup, proportional to its districts; no state is painted whole just because data exists.',
  'how.house.3': 'Seats are apportioned among states by the 2020 census (435 in total) and a majority is 218. In nine states (AL, CA, FL, LA, NC, OH, TN, TX and UT) the district lines were redrawn for 2026; that is why districts are squares, not a geographic map.',
  'how.house.4': 'The reporting percentage follows each state’s unit. In Indiana it is one statewide number repeated for every district; in Texas each contest reports its own. Votes and percentages come from the source, per candidate; a candidate’s percentage is of the votes counted so far. “% reporting” is the share of units (precincts, counties or towns, depending on the state) that have reported: it is not the share of votes.',
  'how.house.5': 'Leading only says who has the most votes right now. “Projected” is an outlet’s estimate, which we cite with a link. “Official” comes from state authorities and can still change. “Certified” requires the state’s document. A 100% count is none of the three.',
  'how.house.6': 'Districts in states no integrated source covers show as “Data unavailable”, with a link to the official portal. Today the count source covers 22 states; see the Data tab.',
  'learn.tab.how': 'How we read results',
  'how.defs.title': 'What each situation means',
  'srcx.title': 'Sources evaluated and why we use (or do not use) each one',
  'srcx.lede': 'Checked on 2026-11-05 from this project’s server. We prioritize official state data; when a source lacks a contest we look for another before showing “no data” — and if none has it we show the official link, never an estimated number.',
  'srcx.oa': 'Open America: counts from the election offices of 22 states (Senate: 9 contests; governor: 9; House: 155 districts). In use. It never projects or declares a winner.',
  'srcx.ap': 'Associated Press: the press standard for projections, but its results API is paid and needs a contract and key. Without one it is not used. If licensed, it enters through /declarations.json with its evidence.',
  'srcx.cbs': 'CBS News: offers no public results API. It is cited only as the source of poll-closing times. Its projections can be recorded in /declarations.json, with a link.',
  'srcx.oe': 'OpenElections: gathers official results on a volunteer basis and only after states publish them; it is not a live-count source. No verified 2026 results are available there.',
  'srcx.states': 'State offices: the primary source. In today’s test Illinois, Ohio, Colorado, Iowa and Oklahoma refused automated access (HTTP 403) and we respect that; Michigan, Kentucky, Alabama and Montana did not respond; Massachusetts had only the primaries published. So those states show “Data unavailable” with a link to the official portal.',
  'srcx.decl': 'Projections, official results and certifications: they only enter through /declarations.json, and every item needs an organization, an https link and a publication time; incomplete items are discarded. The file ships empty: nothing is inferred from the count.',
  'src.decl.name': 'Projections, official results and certifications (declarations.json file)',
  'src.decl.provides': 'Who projected, declared or certified each winner, with the organization, the evidence link and the time. It comes from a file kept by the site’s team; vote counts are never used to infer a winner.',
